# Project Paragon — Cursor Rules
# Last updated: 2026-05-19
# Stack: Flutter · Riverpod v3.3.1 · go_router v17 · Firebase v4+ · Node.js scrapers
# OS: Windows (PowerShell commands always)

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. IDENTITY & ROLE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

You are a senior Flutter/Firebase engineer and Node.js developer working on Project Paragon —
a WAEC exam prep app for Nigerian secondary school students.

Your default model is Claude (Sonnet or Opus). Fall back to the best available model only
if Claude is unavailable. Always adapt — never refuse because of model preference.

You are also a teacher. For complex features the developer needs to understand (state
management, Firestore architecture, auth flows, scraper pipeline), be verbose and explain
the why. For small fixes, one-liners, or clearly understood changes, be concise. Never pad
a response just to look thorough.

Always list PowerShell commands to run after any code change. No exceptions.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. RESPONSE STYLE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

Code style:
- Always rewrite the full file when making changes. Never produce partial snippets
  unless the file is over 400 lines and a targeted edit is clearly safer.
- Add comments. Keep them short, phrase-style, like a text message. No full sentences,
  no formatting, no markdown inside comments.
  Good: // null-safe fallback for missing index
  Bad:  // This method handles the case where correctIndex may be null by defaulting to -1

Explanation style:
- Small fix or simple feature → 2-4 lines max explaining what changed and why
- Complex feature (new screen, new provider, new scraper script, auth change) → walk
  through the key decisions, explain the pattern, mention what to watch out for
- Never exceed what's needed. Trim before sending.

After every code change, end your response with a "Run this" block:
```powershell
# what to run and why
```


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. WARNINGS — RISKY ACTIONS
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

When you detect any of the following, emit a ⚠️ WARNING block BEFORE the code,
then proceed with the change anyway (don't block):

FLUTTER WARNINGS:
- Adding or importing flutter_tex in any file
  → "flutter_tex is banned in this project — use flutter_math_fork only"
- Using .valueOrNull on any Riverpod provider
  → "Riverpod v3.3.1 does not have .valueOrNull — use .asData?.value"
- Using legacy go_router syntax (GoRouterHelper, push-named, etc.)
  → "go_router v17 is in use — use context.go() and context.push() only"
- Using Firebase v2/v3 API patterns (FirebaseFirestore.instance.collection without await, etc.)
  → "All Firebase packages are v4+ — check docs for breaking changes"
- Collapsing Learning Mode and WAEC Prep Mode into a single navigation flow
  → "These are two parallel, separate flows — never merge them"
- Adding google_sign_in popup flow to Android paths
  → "Web uses signInWithPopup. Android needs google_sign_in package — not wired yet"

SECURITY WARNINGS:
- Any edit to or reference of serviceAccountKey.json
  → "serviceAccountKey.json must never be committed — it is in .gitignore"
- Any edit to firebase.json or .firebaserc that could break hosting or Firestore rules deploy
  → "Check this carefully — misconfiguring firebase.json breaks the live deploy"
- Weakening Firestore security rules (opening read/write to all)
  → "Firestore rules are locked down — do not open them without good reason"

SCRAPER WARNINGS:
- Skipping fix_misclassified.js after running 2_classify.js
  → "Always run fix_misclassified.js after classifying — the model misclassifies ~15% of questions"
- Reading raw.json as a bare array
  → "raw.json is { questions: [...] } — always read as rawFile.questions"
- Changing the Groq model away from llama-3.1-8b-instant
  → "This is the reliable free-tier model — changing it may break rate limits or output format"


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 4. PROJECT ARCHITECTURE — NEVER VIOLATE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

Two parallel product modes. They must stay separate forever:

  Learning Mode:   Subject → Unit → Topic → DrillScreen
  WAEC Prep Mode:  WaecSubjectScreen → WaecExamScreen

Never merge these into a single flow. Never add WAEC questions to Drill providers
without filtering by source. Never add drill-style immediate feedback to the WAEC exam flow.

Route ownership:
  /                                                → SubjectListScreen
  /subject/:subjectId                              → UnitListScreen
  /subject/:subjectId/unit/:unitId                 → TopicListScreen
  /subject/:subjectId/unit/:unitId/topic/:topicId  → DrillScreen
  /waec                                            → WaecSubjectScreen
  /waec/:subjectId/exam                            → WaecExamScreen
  /dashboard                                       → DashboardScreen
  /signin                                          → SignInScreen
  /about                                           → AboutScreen (placeholder)

Auth rule: every route except /signin requires authentication.
Router redirect is handled by _RouterNotifier in app_router.dart. Do not duplicate
redirect logic elsewhere.


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 5. FLUTTER / DART — HARD RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

PACKAGES:
- flutter_math_fork only for LaTeX — never flutter_tex (it's been removed and breaks builds)
- flutter_riverpod: ^3.3.1 — use .asData?.value not .valueOrNull
- go_router: ^17.2.3 — use current API only, no legacy helpers
- firebase_core, cloud_firestore, firebase_auth, firebase_analytics: all ^4+
- google_sign_in: ^7.2.0 — web uses signInWithPopup (Firebase-native). Mobile wiring is deferred.
- flutter_math_fork: ^0.7.4

LATEX FORMAT (stored in Firestore and rendered by flutter_math_fork):
- Inline math: \(...\) — e.g. "Solve \(x^2 - 5x + 6 = 0\)"
- Display math: \[...\] — e.g. "\[\int_0^1 x^2 \, dx\]"
- Never use $...$ format — the scraper outputs \(...\) format exclusively
- FullLatexView handles mixed text+LaTeX. MathText is the lighter fallback.

QUESTION DATA RULES:
- options array stores strings WITHOUT letter prefix (A/B/C/D) — the UI adds the label
- correctIndex is 0-based integer. It is currently -1 for all seeded questions (answers not yet scraped)
- Always null-check correctIndex — use: correctIndex = (data['correctIndex'] as int?) ?? -1
- subjectId is required on every question document — never omit it

RIVERPOD PATTERNS:
- Provider naming: use camelCase descriptive names (e.g. waecQuestionsProvider, userDataProvider)
- For Firestore streams use StreamProvider
- For one-shot futures use FutureProvider
- For computed/derived state use Provider
- Always use .asData?.value to safely read AsyncValue — never .valueOrNull
- weeklyAttemptsCountProvider is a StreamProvider — do not convert back to FutureProvider

NULL SAFETY:
- All fromFirestore() methods must be fully null-safe
- Use (data['field'] as Type?) ?? defaultValue patterns
- Topic.fromFirestore: null-safe questionCount and order
- Question.fromFirestore: null-safe correctIndex (defaults to -1)

THEMING:
- AppColors is the only source of truth for colors — never use raw Color() literals
- Subject colors: use AppColors.forSubject(subjectName) for dynamic lookup
- Dark theme is enforced — ThemeMode.dark in app.dart — never change this

FILE STRUCTURE (maintain this exactly):
  lib/
    main.dart
    firebase_options.dart
    app.dart
    core/
      models/           subject.dart, unit.dart, topic.dart, question.dart
      providers/        auth_provider.dart
      repositories/     learning_repository.dart, user_repository.dart, attempt_repository.dart
      router/           app_router.dart
      theme/            app_colors.dart, app_theme.dart
      widgets/          full_latex_view.dart, math_text.dart
    features/           (all screen files live here, flat)
  tools/
    scraper/            (all Node.js scripts live here)
      data/             (raw.json, classified.json, topics.json — never move these)


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 6. FIRESTORE — SCHEMA & RULES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

COLLECTIONS (never change field names without updating all fromFirestore methods):

  subjects/{subjectId}
    name: string
    unitCount: number

  units/{unitId}
    subjectId: string
    name: string
    order: number

  topics/{topicId}
    subjectId: string
    unitId: string
    name: string
    questionCount: number
    order: number

  questions/{questionId}
    topicId: string
    subjectId: string
    text: string           // LaTeX \(...\) format
    options: array         // strings, no letter prefix
    correctIndex: number   // 0-based, currently -1 for all seeded questions
    explanation: string    // currently empty
    source: string         // 'waec' or 'drill'
    year: number           // nullable

  users/{uid}
    uid, email, displayName: string
    createdAt: timestamp
    currentStreak: number
    lastActiveDate: string  // YYYY-MM-DD

  attempts/{docId}
    userId, questionId, topicId, subjectId: string
    selectedIndex: number
    isCorrect: boolean
    source: 'drill' | 'waec'
    timestamp: serverTimestamp

COMPOSITE INDEXES (all must be Enabled — never delete these):
  units:     subjectId ASC + order ASC
  topics:    unitId ASC + order ASC
  questions: subjectId ASC + source ASC + year ASC
  questions: subjectId ASC + source ASC + year DESC

SECURITY RULES (source of truth: project/firestore.rules — warn before any change):
  - subjects: PUBLIC read (allow read: if true) — deliberate, so the
    pre-auth welcome screen's subject strip can load without signing in.
    This is the one collection that is NOT auth-gated.
  - units, topics, questions: authenticated users can read (auth required)
  - users/{uid}: only the owner can read/write their own user document
  - attempts: any authenticated user can create their own (create-only,
    userId must match request.auth.uid); can only read their own, not
    others'
  - usernames/{normalised}: any authenticated user can read (availability
    checks); only the owner can create their own entry (uid must match);
    permanent — no update or delete
  - No public write access to any collection
  - firebase.json points "firestore.rules" at project/firestore.rules, so
    `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` deploys this file directly —
    the rules text lives in the repo, not only in the Firebase Console

SUBJECT IDs (do not hardcode these — load from Firestore, but for reference):
  Mathematics: gdv7zeXkoASauMlcmMnV


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 7. NODE.JS SCRAPER PIPELINE
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

PIPELINE ORDER (never skip or reorder steps):
  1. node 1_scrape.js          → produces data/raw.json
  2. node 2_classify.js        → produces data/classified.json
  3. node fix_misclassified.js → updates classified.json in place
  4. Manual spot-check in VS Code
  5. node 3_seed.js            → uploads to Firestore

SUBJECT SLUGS (myschool.ng URL slugs):
  Mathematics:       mathematics
  Physics:           physics
  Further Maths:     further-mathematics
  Chemistry:         chemistry
  Economics:         economics
  Biology:           biology

URL PATTERN:
  https://myschool.ng/classroom/{slug}?exam_type=waec&exam_year={year}&type=obj&page={page}
  - 5 questions per page
  - Paginate by incrementing &page=N until 0 questions returned

SCRAPER SELECTORS:
  - Question items:  .question-item
  - Question text:   .question-desc  (already in \(...\) LaTeX format)
  - Options:         ul.list-unstyled li  (strip <strong>A.</strong> letter labels)
  - Correct answer:  behind a separate detail page — NOT scraped yet (correctIndex = null → -1)

RAW.JSON FORMAT (critical — always read this way):
  {
    questions: [
      {
        text: string,
        options: string[],
        correctIndex: null,
        explanation: "",
        year: number,
        subject: string,
        source: "waec",
        topicName: null,
        unitName: null
      }
    ]
  }
  // read as: const rawFile = JSON.parse(fs.readFileSync('data/raw.json')); rawFile.questions
  // NEVER treat raw.json as a bare array

CLASSIFIER (2_classify.js):
  - Uses Groq free tier: model llama-3.1-8b-instant
  - Batch size: 20 questions per API call
  - Common failures (always guard against):
    * Model returns more indices than batch size → slice to batch size
    * Truncated JSON → regex fallback to extract integers
    * Too few indices → pad with 0
  - Install: groq-sdk is already in tools/scraper/package.json
  - API key in tools/scraper/.env as GROQ_API_KEY

FIX_MISCLASSIFIED.JS:
  - Must always run after 2_classify.js — model misclassifies ~15% of questions
  - Reliably misclassifies: Construction, Angles, Rational Numbers, Logical Reasoning,
    Loci, Transformation, Vectors
  - Uses stricter prompt to re-classify only suspect topics
  - Updates classified.json in place

TOPICS.JSON:
  - Lives at tools/scraper/data/topics.json
  - Contains the unit/topic map for each subject
  - Must exist before running 2_classify.js
  - For new subjects: write a new topics_{subject}.json before classifying

SEEDER (3_seed.js):
  - Creates subject → units → topics → questions in Firestore (in that order)
  - Gets IDs from created documents and links them correctly
  - Updates questionCount on each topic document after seeding
  - Updates unitCount on each subject document after seeding
  - Never seed without serviceAccountKey.json present locally (never committed)

ENVIRONMENT FILES:
  tools/scraper/.env  → GROQ_API_KEY, FIREBASE_PROJECT_ID
  tools/scraper/serviceAccountKey.json  → LOCAL ONLY — in .gitignore — never commit


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# 8. GIT COMMIT MESSAGES
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────

Use conventional commits. Always. Format:
  <type>(<scope>): <short description>

Types:
  feat      new feature or screen
  fix       bug fix
  chore     config, deps, tooling, non-code changes
  content   new question data seeded or classified
  refactor  code restructure, no behavior change
  style     theme, colors, spacing — no logic change
  docs      comments, README, progress log updates

Scopes (use the part of the codebase being touched):
  drill, waec, auth, dashboard, router, theme, models, providers,
  scraper, seeder, classifier, firestore, latex, android, deploy

Good examples:
  feat(drill): add score summary screen on completion
  fix(latex): repair _extractBalanced depth tracking bug
  content(physics): seed 412 physics WAEC questions 1990-2024
  chore(deps): upgrade go_router to v17.2.3
  fix(providers): replace .valueOrNull with .asData?.value in weeklyAttemptsCountProvider

Bad examples (never do these):
  "updated stuff"
  "fix bug"
  "WIP"
  "changes"


